  "narration": "On slide 15, we discuss the concept that the value proposition remains a hypothesis until real behavior supports it. A value proposition is a statement that describes the value a product or service provides to the customer. It's essentially an answer to the question, ‘Why should someone buy my product or service?’

At this stage in the design thinking process, your value proposition is a hypothesis. It's a prediction about what the market will think about your product or service and its value. However, just like when you write a hypothesis in science experiments, you can't prove it right or wrong. It’s a guess based on what you know about your target audience and your product.

The key point here is that real behavior is what validates your hypothesis. Real behavior is the actual actions and decisions your target users make with your product or service. When you observe and listen to how your users interact with your product, you can see whether they find value in it. This real-world evidence can confirm or contradict your initial hypothesis. So, before you finalize your value proposition as a definitive statement, you need to gather real user feedback and their actual interactions with your product. This process helps you refine your understanding of what value your product provides and whether you're on the right track."
}
